  // The narrative that leads the page. On trading days this is the DAY's wrap (dailyWrap);
  // on Saturdays it is the WEEK's wrap (weeklyWrap) and dailyWrap stays in place behind it.
  dailyWrap: {
    label: "The Day That Was",
    kicker: "Daily Wrap",
    headline: "Nifty leaps 576 points to 24,537, its best session in seven months, as crude slips below $100 and Financials, Auto and Banks carry the Sensex 2,009 points higher \u2014 Media and Energy the only sectors left behind",
    summary: "The whole move was made overnight. Brent fell below $100 a barrel after Democrats swept the US midterms and the odds of a West Asia settlement improved, US equities rallied, and the rupee steadied near 94.46 a dollar against 94.64 on Wednesday. Indian equities opened at the top of that news and simply stayed there: the Nifty opened at 24,378.05, dipped to 24,296.30 and closed at 24,536.60, up 576.00 points or 2.40%, its highest close since early August and its biggest single-day gain since 8 April. The Sensex ran from 78,910.94 to a 79,465.03 high and settled at 79,310.01, up 2,008.61 points or 2.60%. That is six consecutive sessions and roughly 8% for both benchmarks \u2014 the longest winning run since November 2025 \u2014 and it took BSE-listed market capitalisation past Rs 501 lakh crore for the first time, adding about Rs 7.7 lakh crore of investor wealth in a single day. What makes this different from the last five sessions is who did the buying. This was a financials and cyclicals day: Nifty Capital Markets +4.24%, Auto +4.14% to 27,601.75, Financial Services +4.01%, Realty +3.52%, Private Bank +3.44%, Bank +3.33% to 57,773.65 and PSU Bank +3.13%, with Consumer Durables and IT +2.42% behind them. Bajaj Finance led the Sensex at +7.65%, followed by M&M +5.59%, Maruti +5.48%, IndusInd Bank +5.35% and ICICI Bank +5.33%, while NTPC, Bharat Electronics, Adani Ports, ITC and Bharti Airtel (-0.52%) gave ground. Only two sectors fell: Energy -0.52% as Reliance gave back part of Monday's 11.6% jump, and Media -0.84%; on an equal-weighted basis of the F&O universe, Chemicals was the worst basket of the day at -2.78%, dragged by PI Industries' 19.07% collapse. Breadth was unambiguously positive \u2014 2,868 advances against 1,384 declines and 169 unchanged on BSE, 166 of 216 F&O stocks up \u2014 but the smaller the market cap, the smaller the gain: Next 50 +1.38%, Midcap 150 +1.93%, Smallcap 250 +0.79%, Smallcap 100 +0.56%, against the Nifty 500's +2.00% to 23,224.70. That inversion is the honest read on the day. When the bid comes from crude, the currency and foreign flows, it lands on the largest, most liquid balance sheets, and it leaves the tail behind; the BSE SME IPO index's +1.99% is the exception, and it is a 159-stock index with 126 risers rather than a broad small-cap move. Two numbers argue for caution. India VIX closed at 15.58, up about 2.6% on a day the Nifty rose 2.4%, which is not what a genuine de-risking looks like, and Kotak Neo's tape printed 15.89 at the same moment, so even the level is soft. And the benchmarks are still down 6.1% and 6.9% for 2026. FIIs bought Rs 7,830.40 crore of equities on NSDL's count, the sixth straight net-buy session; whether that flow survives a crude rebound is the only question that matters for Friday.",
    stats: ["Nifty 2.40%", "Sensex 2.60%"],
    statsLabel: "Day"
  },

