    { date: "2026-10-30", label: "Fri 30 Oct", niftyClose: 23053.10, niftyChangePct: 0.51, sensexChangePct: 0.42, note: "A second straight gain, but a hollow one. The Sensex rose 308.49 points (+0.42%) to 74,459.53 and the Nifty 116.35 points (+0.51%) to 23,053.10, after opening flat and running to intraday highs of 74,742.59 and 23,118.10 respectively; the Nifty's low was 22,905.10. Two sectors did all the buying. Nifty IT rose 2.32% to 32,606.90 as global money rotated out of the AI-infrastructure trade — Infosys +3.36% to Rs 1,185, HCLTech +1.72%, Wipro and Coforge more than 4% — and Pharma added 1.55% with Healthcare 1.58%, Laurus Labs +7.20% on profit more than doubling and Zydus Lifesciences +5.16%. Trent led the Nifty with a 5.44% jump to Rs 3,017.30 on a 25% rise in Q2 net profit to Rs 468 crore and 22% revenue growth, followed by IndiGo (+4.33%), Infosys (+3.36%), Apollo Hospitals (+3.02%) and Power Grid (+1.92%). Shriram Finance (-2.13%), Nestle India (-1.82%), M&M (-1.72%), Jio Financial (-1.21%) and Adani Ports (-1.18%) led the losers. Consumer Durables (-1.78%), Media (-1.46%), Energy (-0.97%), PSU Bank (-0.93%), Auto (-0.87%), Realty (-0.72%) and FMCG (-0.62%) all fell. The broader market was sold hard: Midcap 150 -0.93% to 21,047.50, Smallcap 250 -1.97% to 16,764.55, Midcap 100 -0.93% to 57,301.25, Smallcap 100 -1.85% to 18,463.60, Next 50 -0.61% to 67,709.10, Nifty 500 -0.21% to 21,895.90, BSE SME IPO -1.41% to 1,12,571.49. NSE breadth was 1,013 advances against 2,511 declines and 109 unchanged out of 3,633, with 60 stocks at 52-week highs and 211 at lows. India VIX rose 1.22% to 15.30 on NSE's official close file, Brent jumped about 2% to roughly $117.8 on fresh attacks on Saudi energy infrastructure and a tanker hit in the Strait of Hormuz, and the rupee firmed to about 94.95 a dollar, its best month since February. (Two figures in an earlier version of this row were corrected against NSE's and BSE's own closing data: the BSE SME IPO index closed at 1,12,571.49, not the intraday level first carried, and India VIX at 15.30, not 15.52.)" },
    { date: "2026-11-02", label: "Mon 2 Nov", niftyClose: 23440.80, niftyChangePct: 1.68, sensexChangePct: 1.66, note: "The day Reliance and crude did the work. The Sensex opened at 75,178.24, dipped to 74,978.12 and closed at its session high of 75,692.78, up 1,233.25 points or 1.66%; the Nifty went 23,222.30 to a 23,207.05 low and closed at its high of 23,440.80, up 387.70 points or 1.68%. Reliance Industries was the story — +11.60% to Rs 1,300.00, its biggest single-day gain since April 2020, after September-quarter net profit attributable to owners of Rs 25,548 crore (+41% year on year) on a near-28% rise in gross revenue, with the oil-to-chemicals segment riding global fuel margins and Jio Platforms adding subscribers. Brent for December fell $5.01, or 4.42%, to $108.42 a barrel and WTI 4.23% to $99.72 as Iranian and Qatari officials held a third day of talks to end the US-Israeli war with Iran. Sectorally that produced the exact inverse of Friday: Oil & Gas +5.95%, Energy +3.92%, Infra +3.70%, Metal +3.21%, Consumer Durables +2.70%, PSU Bank +1.36%, Auto +1.33%, Realty +1.03%, FMCG +0.84% and Bank +0.58% (55,002.20, +315.25), against Pharma -0.32%, Private Bank -0.40%, IT -0.50% (32,442.50) and Media -0.76%. Tata Steel +5.83% to Rs 177.37, JSW Steel +5.13% to Rs 1,309.90, Coal India +4.88% to Rs 408.00 on record October supplies of 70.3 million tonnes, and Shriram Finance +4.88% to Rs 863.35 followed Reliance; Infosys -2.03% to Rs 1,161.00, ICICI Bank -0.76%, Dr Reddy's -0.74%, Kotak Mahindra Bank -0.70% and Axis Bank -0.66% led the fallers as global money kept leaving the AI-infrastructure trade (Nasdaq -2.86% on Friday, Intel -28.6%). The broader market joined in for once: Next 50 +1.85% to 68,962.65, Smallcap 250 +1.35% to 16,991.00, Midcap 150 +1.25% to 21,310.75, Midcap 100 +1.19% to 57,983.40, Smallcap 100 +0.71% to 18,595.30 and Nifty 500 +1.58% to 22,241.40 — but the BSE SME IPO index closed flat to lower at 1,12,547.00 (-0.02%) after trading above 1,13,450 in the morning. NSE breadth was 2,503 advances against 1,900 declines and 203 unchanged; FIIs bought Rs 3,114.68 crore net and DIIs sold Rs 392.20 crore; India VIX rose 1.40% to 15.52 and the rupee closed at 94.90 a dollar." },
    { date: "2026-11-03", label: "Tue 3 Nov", niftyClose: 23810.85, niftyChangePct: 1.58, sensexChangePct: 1.62, note: "The rotation reversed, and both benchmarks closed at their highs. The Sensex opened at 76,089.72, never traded below that, and closed at 76,917.85, up 1,225.07 points or 1.62%; the Nifty went 23,582.25 to a 23,483.00 low and closed at 23,810.85, up 370.05 points or 1.58% — two-month highs on both, and a fourth straight gain. Monday's crude-short trades did not extend: Brent recovered about 1% to $105.60 a barrel after Monday's 7.6% slide to $104.77, so Oil & Gas went from +5.95% to flat and Metal from +3.21% to -0.14%. What replaced them was consumption and financials. Nifty FMCG rose 3.65% to 45,580 on Marico +8.77% to Rs 868.60 (September-quarter net profit up 31% to Rs 550 crore, FY27 guidance raised to mid-20s EBITDA growth), Britannia +6.36%, Nestle India +4.47%, Tata Consumer +4.29%, Radico Khaitan +3.66%, Colgate +3.60%, Hindustan Unilever +3.40% and ITC +2.82% despite an 18.7% fall in its own quarterly profit. Realty +2.33% to 841.40, Private Bank +1.98%, Financial Services +1.74%, Auto +1.71%, Infra +1.10% to 9,225.40, Bank +0.98% to 55,540.05 and Media +0.49% followed. The laggards were PSU Bank -1.27% to 8,241.10 — SBI fell 1.70% to Rs 970.25 even after a 14.28% rise in net profit to Rs 23,038 crore — with Energy -0.11%, Metal -0.14%, and Infosys -1.85% to Rs 1,139.50 the Nifty's single biggest drag on the day its Rs 26 interim dividend went ex. Two policy lines ran underneath: Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman said the government would consider reviewing capital gains taxes on foreign investors, and reports said SEBI had recommended scrapping long-term capital gains tax for foreign equity investors. The broader market underperformed for a second session — Midcap 150 +0.84%, Smallcap 250 +0.43%, Midcap 100 +1.06% to 58,596.25, Nifty 500 +1.25% to 22,520.35, Next 50 +0.96% to 69,624.35, BSE SME IPO +0.47% to 1,13,071.00. India VIX rose 1.42% to 15.72 on a 1.58% up-day, NSE breadth was 2,503 advances against 1,900 declines and 203 unchanged, and the rupee settled 13 paise stronger at 94.71 a dollar. In the Nifty 500 the extremes were earnings-driven: KRBL +10.59% and Trident +7.11% on profit beats, against KEC International -9.36% on an 82% fall in profit, GNFC -8.53%, MOIL -7.97% and Sheela Foam -6.98%." },
    { date: "2026-11-04", label: "Wed 4 Nov", niftyClose: 23960.60, niftyChangePct: 0.63, sensexChangePct: 0.50, note: "The fifth straight gain and the widest one: the Nifty closed at 23,960.60, up 149.75 points, having been down to 23,696.15 in the morning, and the Sensex added 383.55 to 77,301.40. Media +5.15% on Saregama's 14.91% post-results jump, Realty +2.85%, Metal +2.80% and Consumer Durables +2.04% led, and for the first time this week the Midcap 150 (+1.60%) and Smallcap 250 (+1.97%) beat the Nifty. IT was the only loser at -1.50%, with TCS -2.52% and Infosys -2.89%. NSE breadth 2,720 advances to 884 declines; VIX -3.24% to 15.21; Brent -0.73% to $102.80." },
    { date: "2026-11-05", label: "Thu 5 Nov", niftyClose: 24536.60, niftyChangePct: 2.40, sensexChangePct: 2.60, note: "The biggest single-day gain in seven months, and the sixth straight advance \u2014 the longest winning run since November 2025. The Nifty opened at 24,378.05, dipped to 24,296.30 and closed at 24,536.60, up 576.00 points or 2.40%, its highest close since early August; the Sensex ran from 78,910.94 to a 79,465.03 high and settled at 79,310.01, up 2,008.61 points or 2.60%. The trigger was overnight: Brent slid below $100 a barrel as Democrats swept the US midterms and hopes of a West Asia settlement hardened, US equities rallied, and foreign money kept arriving. The leadership was entirely financial and cyclical \u2014 Nifty Capital Markets +4.24%, Auto +4.14% to 27,601.75, Financial Services +4.01%, Realty +3.52% to 895.90, Private Bank +3.44%, Bank +3.33% to 57,773.65 and PSU Bank +3.13%, with Consumer Durables and IT +2.42% to 32,837.85 behind them. Only two sectors fell: Energy -0.52% to 36,884.45 on Reliance giving back part of Monday's 11.6% and Media -0.84%, while Chemicals was the day's worst basket on an equal-weighted basis. Bajaj Finance was the Sensex's best at +7.65%, with M&M +5.59%, Maruti +5.48%, IndusInd Bank +5.35% and ICICI Bank +5.33%; NTPC, Bharat Electronics, Adani Ports, ITC and Bharti Airtel (-0.52%) lagged. The broader market joined in but lagged the headline: Next 50 +1.38% to 71,768.65, Midcap 150 +1.93% to 22,290.40, Smallcap 250 +0.79% to 17,535.70, Midcap 100 +1.87%, Smallcap 100 +0.56%, Nifty 500 +2.00% to 23,224.70, and the BSE SME IPO index +1.99% to 1,15,864.00. Breadth on BSE was 2,868 advances against 1,384 declines and 169 unchanged; 126 of the 159 SME constituents rose. BSE-listed market capitalisation crossed Rs 501 lakh crore for the first time, adding roughly Rs 7.7 lakh crore of investor wealth in a session (newsdrum puts the gain at Rs 8.2 lakh crore). FIIs were net buyers of Rs 7,830.40 crore of equities on NSDL's numbers, against Rs 7,284.03 crore on NSE's provisional file; the rupee settled at 94.46-94.48 a dollar against 94.64 on Wednesday. India VIX closed at 15.58, up about 2.6% \u2014 it did not fall with the rally, which is the one internal contradiction in the session and the reason several strategists spent the afternoon warning about consolidation; Kotak Neo's tape printed 15.89 at the same moment, so treat the VIX level as approximate. Brent was $96.12 a barrel, up 1.59% on the day after the overnight slide." }
